    {
      "heading": "The Original Heart of Bombay",
      "text": "Fort is where Bombay began. The original walled settlement of the British East India Company - built around the Fort of Good Hope in the 17th century - is now Mumbai's most historically significant commercial and legal district. The walls are long gone, but the name remains, and so does the extraordinary concentration of Victorian Gothic and Art Deco architecture that makes Fort one of the most architecturally significant urban precincts in Asia. Pin code 400001 is not just an address. It is a statement - the most prestigious postal code in India, carrying the weight of over three centuries of Bombay's history."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Institutions That Define India",
      "text": "Fort is home to institutions that define India's legal, financial and cultural life. The Bombay High Court - established in 1862 in a magnificent Victorian Gothic building - is one of the oldest and most respected courts in Asia. The Reserve Bank of India, headquartered in Fort since 1935, sets monetary policy for the world's most populous nation. The State Bank of India, HDFC, ICICI and virtually every major Indian and international bank have their head offices or flagship branches in Fort. The University of Mumbai's Fort campus, the Government Law College and the Asiatic Society Library have educated generations of India's finest minds. And the Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus - a UNESCO World Heritage Site - is one of the world's most magnificent railway stations, handling over 3 million commuters every day."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Kala Ghoda - Mumbai's Cultural Soul",
      "text": "Within Fort lies Kala Ghoda - Mumbai's premier art district and one of the most culturally alive precincts in Asia. Named after the black equestrian statue of King Edward VII that once stood here (now removed), Kala Ghoda is home to the Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya (the Prince of Wales Museum), the Jehangir Art Gallery, the National Gallery of Modern Art and dozens of private galleries, design boutiques and cultural spaces. The annual Kala Ghoda Arts Festival - nine days of visual arts, music, dance, theatre, cinema, literature and heritage walks - is Mumbai's most celebrated cultural event, drawing hundreds of thousands of visitors every February. Fort without Kala Ghoda would be like Paris without the Left Bank."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Living and Working in Fort",
      "text": "Fort is primarily a commercial and institutional district - its daytime population of hundreds of thousands dwarfs its small residential community. But those who do live in Fort - the Parsi families in their heritage buildings, the advocates with chambers and homes within walking distance of the High Court, the artists and gallery owners of Kala Ghoda - would not trade their address for anything in Mumbai. The connectivity is unmatched: CST and Churchgate within walking distance, Metro Line 3 at CST, BEST buses covering every corner of the city. The food is legendary: Britannia & Co. in Ballard Estate, the Irani cafés near Flora Fountain, the Kala Ghoda café scene. And the sheer beauty of the built environment - the Victorian Gothic spires, the Art Deco facades, the wide boulevards of Ballard Estate - makes Fort one of the most extraordinary places to live and work in the world. Mumbai96 is proud to serve Fort's community - from the advocate who needs a reliable plumber for her heritage flat to the banker who wants the best dentist near his Ballard Estate office."
    }
  ],
